pacted or put together as to be impermeable or impervious to air, gas, rain, water, etc.: as, a water-''tight'' tank; an air-''tight'' vessel. (''b'') Stanch; strong; firmly built or made.

{{smaller block|'Tis known my father hath no less<br>Than three great argosies; besides two galliases,<br>And twelve ''tight'' galleys. ''Shak.'', T. of the S., ii. 1. 381.}}

{{smaller block|Some ''tight'' vessel that holds out against wind and water.
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Naomi and Ruth.}}}}

Hence—2. Trim; tidy; neat.

{{smaller block|How the ''tight'' lass knives, combs, and scissors spies,<br>And looks on thimbles with desiring eyes.
{{right|''Gay'', Shepherd's Week, Saturday, l. 77.}}}}

{{smaller block|O, 'tis a snug little island!<br>A right little, ''tight'' little island!
{{right|''Dibdin'', The Snug Little Island.}}}}

{{smaller block|A ''tight'', likely wench she was, too.
{{right|''H. B. Stowe'', Uncle Tom's Cabin, viii.}}}}

3. Expert; handy; skilful; adroit; capable.

{{smaller block|My queen 's a squire<br>More ''tight'' at this than thou.
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., iv. 4. 15.}}}}

{{smaller block|And so the house is haunted, is it? It will take a ''tighter'' workman than I am to keep the spirits out of the seven gables. ''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, xiii.}}

4. Close; firm: as, a ''tight'' grasp; a ''tight'' knot.—5. Close-fitting; especially, fitting too closely because too small, narrow, or the like: as, a ''tight'' shoe; a ''tight'' coat.

{{smaller block|A man will always be more looked at whose dress flutters in the air than he whose dress sits ''tight'' upon him.
{{right|''Landor'', Imag. Conv., Archdeacon Hare and Walter [Landor.}}}}

{{smaller block|A wedding-ring growing always ''tighter'' as I grow fatter and older. ''Trollope'', Last Chronicle of Barset, xxxv.}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
6. Close-fisted; narrow; niggardly; parsimonious: as, a man ''tight'' in his dealings. [Colloq.]—7. Tense; taut; strained or stretched so as to leave no slack: as, a ''tight'' rope.

{{smaller block|Nor would he loose the reins, nor could he hold 'em ''tight.''
{{right|''Addison'', tr. of Ovid's Metamorph., ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|Tom has eaten kidney and pigeon pie, and imbibed coffee, till his little skin is as ''tight'' as a drum.
{{right|''T. Hughes'', Tom Brown at Rugby, i. 4.}}}}

8. Produced by or requiring great straining or exertion; severe: as, to get through by a ''tight'' pull; specifically, in ''med.'', noting a cough accompanied with a painful sense of constriction, and without expectoration; racking; hacking. [Colloq.]—9. Scarce; not easily obtained or obtainable, because held firmly or tied up in some way: applied to money; hence, straitened for want of money: as, a ''tight'' money-market. [Commercial slang.]

{{smaller block|A few curt sentences . . . told how matters stood in the City;—money was ''tight;'' . . . but of that financial sensitiveness that shrinks timidly from all enterprise after a period of crash and bankruptcy Culduff could make nothing.
{{right|''Lever'', Bramleighs of Bishop's Folly, I. xxi.}}}}

{{smaller block|I've known the City now for more than ten years, Mr. Crosbie, and I never knew money to be so ''tight'' as it is at this moment.
{{right|''Trollope'', Last Chronicle of Barset, xlii.}}}}

10. Under the influence of strong drink; intoxicated; tipsy; "full." [Slang.]

{{smaller block|No, sir, not a bit tipsy; . . . not even what Mr. Cutbill calls ''tight.''
{{right|''Lever'', Bramleighs of Bishop's Folly, I. xxiv.}}}}

{{smaller block|How she cried out half her sight,
When you staggered by next night,
Twice as dirty as a serpent, and a hundred times as ''tight.''
{{right|''W. Carleton'', Johnny Rich.}}}}

